## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. Change is determined by `===` comparison — and in JavaScript, objects and functions are compared by **reference**, not value.

```jsx
// New object created every render → child re-renders every time
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// New function created every render → same problem
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Primitives (`string`, `number`, `boolean`) are compared by value, so they're safe. Anything heap-allocated is not.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component to skip re-rendering if props haven't changed (shallow equality). Only useful if the parent re-renders frequently and the child is expensive.

```jsx
const Chart = React.memo(({ data }) => <ExpensiveChart data={data} />);
```

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders. Use when passing callbacks as props to memoized children, or as dependencies in `useEffect`/`useMemo`.

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value. Use for expensive calculations or to stabilize object/array references passed as props.

```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme, fontSize }), [theme, fontSize]);
```

**Rule of thumb:** don't reach for these by default. Profile first. Memoization has overhead — it's only a win when the avoided re-render costs more than the memo bookkeeping.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer of a context re-renders when the context value changes. The classic mistake is constructing the value inline:

```jsx
// Bad: new object every render → all consumers re-render
<AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, login, logout }}>
```

Fix it by memoizing the value:

```jsx
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, login, logout }), [user, login, logout]);
<AuthContext.Provider value={value}>
```

For high-frequency updates (e.g., a mouse position context), split contexts by update frequency so slow-changing values don't drag fast-changing ones along.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke, even though its `items` prop didn't change:

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const items = ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry'];

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList
        items={items}                          // new array ref every render
        onSelect={(item) => console.log(item)} // new function every render
      />
    </>
  );
}
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` actually changes:

```jsx
const ITEMS = ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']; // stable reference, outside component

function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');

  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => console.log(item), []);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={ITEMS} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(({ items, onSelect }) => {
  // now only re-renders when items or onSelect reference changes
});
```

The key insight: moving `ITEMS` outside the component is often better than `useMemo` inside it — if the value is truly static, it never needs to be recomputed at all.
